On Thu, Apr 19, 2012 at 11:59 AM, carol white <wht_crl at yahoo.com> wrote:
> I can't. I explained that I want to install the packages on a server in my
> home that I can use.
>

You have a system at home that you don't have root access to?

> I am looking for an equivalent of
>
> install.packages("pkg_name", lib.loc = "../my_folder/..")
>
> without interfering with system files.
>

If your R was configured correctly, you could install packages
wherever you wanted. By default, they would go under your home
directory.

The issue is that R is not properly installed. To fix this, you should
install R properly, that is,
sudo rpm install R-devel

If you don't have root access, you can install R from source in your
home directory.
This means not using RPM packages and not deriving any of the benefits thereof.

However, installing R from source may also require some system
dependencies, and for those you'll need root access as well, and
you'll be back to square one. Your best bet is to install all the
dependencies as root from RPM packages.

Dan




